const intro={"part":"vuelta","c":1,"slug":"vuelta-canto-1","title":"The Return Begins: Martín Fierro Sings Again","summary":"The sequel opens with Fierro asking for silence and attention because the earlier story remains unfinished. He returns to singing despite having broken his instrument at the end of the first poem. He argues that the gaucho's song deserves attention, contrasts the reception of learned town poets and rural singers, and declares that his singing offers judgments rather than mere entertainment. He hopes to live and work again, although he carries the memory of his suffering.","people":"Martín Fierro, again the principal singer and first-person narrator; the audience he directly addresses; other singers invoked in his argument.","themes":"The return of the poetic voice; testimony and truth claims; rural versus urban cultural prestige; personal memory; literary continuity between 1872 and 1879.","words":"vigüela (folk spelling for vihuela or a stringed instrument); cantor (singer); pueblero (town-dweller); mansera (plow handle); pial (lasso technique); bagual (untamed horse).","questions":["How does Fierro signal that the earlier story is incomplete?","How can he be singing when he broke his guitar at the end of the first part?","What is the difference between singing for entertainment and singing to express an opinion?","How does the contrast between the pueblero and the gaucho shape the narrator's argument?"],"nums":[396,406,418],"analysis":"The singer explicitly claims that he tells the truth, but this claim is itself part of the literary performance. Readers can attend to the power of his voice without treating each statement as independent evidence. The renewed song creates a bridge between the 1872 departure and the 1879 return; it also foregrounds whose voices are considered culturally authoritative.","link":"/en/library/themes-and-analysis/","linkText":"Themes and literary analysis"};
